<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP is permitted, and that is a deliberate decision rather than
  an oversight.

  The server JotIt syncs to is whatever the user runs at home: a Synology box,
  a Nextcloud instance, a Raspberry Pi. On a home network those are almost
  always reached over plain `http://` on a LAN address, with no certificate
  anybody could obtain for `192.168.1.100`. Android 9 turned cleartext off by
  default, and without this file every one of those users gets a sync that
  fails with `CLEARTEXT communication not permitted` — an error that blames the
  server for something the server never did.

  What this does and does not cost, stated plainly:

  * Anything on the user's own network can see the path of a note travelling
    in cleartext. The password is sent in an HTTP Basic header, which is
    base64, not encryption. That is true of every HTTP WebDAV server ever, and
    blocking cleartext would not make it safer — it would only make the app
    refuse to work.
  * HTTPS is unaffected and is still preferred: nothing here downgrades a
    request to `http`. Typing `https://` in the remote settings keeps TLS.

  To tighten it, replace the base-config flag with a `<domain-config>` naming
  the host that is actually yours. The address is not known at build time,
  which is why it is not hard-coded.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
